<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lohri</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lohṛī</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lohri is a midwinter folk and harvest festival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and other parts of northern India, marking the passing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a> and the end of winter. It falls on the night before Maghi (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makar-sankranti">Makar Sankranti</a>), commonly 13 January, and is celebrated by lighting bonfires, eating festive foods such as jaggery and gajak, singing, and dancing the bhangra and giddha. Many of its songs tell the legend of Dulla Bhatti, set in the reign of the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> and regarded in Punjab as a hero for rescuing girls from being sold in slave markets. European visitors mentioned the festival at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Maharaja Ranjit Singh</a> in the 1830s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lohri.jpg" alt="Lohri" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karen Sandhu · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Happy_Lohri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
